      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Functional assessment of coronary artery stenosis is performed by measuring the fractional flow reserve (FFR) under hyperemic conditions (Adenosine). However, the use of adenosine portends limitations."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "We sought to investigate the relationship and correlation between FFR and the Pd/Pa value obtained just after the intracoronary infusion (acute drop) of nitroglycerin (Pd/Pa-NTG) and if this parameter enhances diagnostic accuracy for FFR prediction compared to the resting baseline Pd/Pa."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We conducted a multicenter study including prospectively patients presenting intermediate coronary artery stenosis (30-70%) evaluated with pressure wire. Resting baseline Pd/Pa, Pd/Pa-NTG and FFR were measured."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "283 patients (335 lesions) were included. Resting baseline Pd/Pa value was 0.72 to 1.0 (0.93\u2009±\u20090.04), Pd/Pa-NTG was 0.60 to 1.0 (0.87\u2009±\u20090.07) and FFR 0.55 to 1.0 (0.83\u2009±\u20090.08). The ROC curves for resting baseline Pd/Pa and for Pd/Pa-NTG, using a FFR\u2009≤\u20090.80 showed an AUC of 0.88 (95% CI: 0.84-0.92, P\u2009<\u20090.001) and 0.94 (95% CI: 0.92-0.96, P\u2009<\u20090.001) respectively. The optimal cutoff values of resting baseline Pd/Pa and Pd/Pa-NTG for an FFR\u2009>\u20090.80, were >0.96 and >0.88, respectively. These values were present in a 29.8% (n\u2009=\u2009100) and a 47.1% (n\u2009=\u2009158), of the total lesions. Scatter plots showed a better correlation and agreement points with Pd/Pa-NTG than resting baseline Pd/Pa. The cutoff value of Pd/Pa-NTG\u2009>\u20090.88 showed an excellent NPV (96.2% for FFR\u2009>\u20090.8 and 100% for FFR\u2009>\u20090.75) and sensitivity (95% for FFR\u2009>\u20090.8 and 100% for FFR\u2009>\u20090.75) which were consistently high across all the subgroups analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The cutoff value of acute Pd/Pa-NTG\u2009>\u20090.88 has a high NPV meaning adenosine-FFR can be avoided in almost half of lesions."
          }
        ]
      },
